Let $(E,\|\cdot\|)$ be a Banach space equipped with a multi-norm $(\|\cdot\|_n : n\in\mathbb{N})$, i.e. a sequence of norms with $\|\cdot\|_n$ defined on $E^n$, satisfying the standard multi-norm axioms (invariance under permutation of coordinates, $\|(x_1,\dots,x_n,0)\|_{n+1}=\|(x_1,\dots,x_n)\|_n$, $\|(x_1,\dots,x_n,x_n)\|_{n+1}=\|(x_1,\dots,x_n)\|_n$, and $\|(\alpha_1 x_1,\dots,\alpha_n x_n)\|_n\le(\max_i|\alpha_i|)\,\|(x_1,\dots,x_n)\|_n$).

A finite family $(F_1,\dots,F_n)$ of closed subspaces of $E$ such that $E=F_1\oplus\cdots\oplus F_n$ is called:
\begin{itemize}
\item an \emph{orthogonal decomposition} of $E$ (with respect to the multi-norm) if for every choice $x_i\in F_i$ one has $\|x_1+\cdots+x_n\| = \|(x_1,\dots,x_n)\|_n$;
\item a \emph{small decomposition} if a strictly stronger associated condition holds (the one called "small" in the standard development of the subject), which is known to imply orthogonality.
\end{itemize}

It is known that every small decomposition of $E$ is orthogonal. 

Question: Is the converse true? That is, must every orthogonal decomposition $(F_1,\dots,F_n)$ of a Banach space $E$ with respect to a multi-norm be a small decomposition?

